@startuml proto-freqmeter-frame
' ===========================================================================
' proto-freqmeter-frame.puml - (b) what a "frame" is here, and the dmem map.
'
' There is no packet in this act, and drawing one would be the wrong picture.
' The incoming PWM IS the frame: a period with a high time inside it, and the
' only payload is the pair of 16-bit numbers the firmware recovers from it. So
' the breakout worth drawing is (1) the two numbers and where they live, and
' (2) the twelve banked points, because the 16-bit claim is only visible when
' the points are laid out together.
' ===========================================================================

title Frequency + duty meter - the two numbers, and the twelve banked points\n<font size=12>no packet: the PWM period IS the frame, and the payload is a 16-bit pair</font>

left to right direction
hide empty description
hide stereotype
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam rectangle {
  BorderColor #33555
  FontName monospaced
  FontSize 12
}
skinparam rectangle<<in>> {
  BackgroundColor #DCE6F5
  BorderColor #5577AA
}
skinparam rectangle<<out>> {
  BackgroundColor #D6F5D6
  BorderColor #3A7A3A
}
skinparam rectangle<<w>> {
  BackgroundColor #EFEFEF
  BorderColor #999999
}
skinparam rectangle<<hi>> {
  BackgroundColor #FFF4D6
  BorderColor #C8B800
}

rectangle "INCOMING PWM\nfrom outside\n\n<b>pad bit 6</b>\nFM_IN" as PW <<in>>
rectangle "T\n<b>dmem[0..1]</b>\n\n16-bit PERIOD\n\nreset at every\nRISING edge\n\n<b>so T is the period\nat the rising edge</b>" as T <<out>>
rectangle "H\n<b>dmem[2..3]</b>\n\n16-bit HIGH TIME\n\nreset at every\nRISING edge\n\n<b>stops counting at the\nFALLING edge</b>" as H <<out>>
rectangle "BANK\n<b>dmem[5]</b> = the slot\n\nT high H low into\nthe pair dmem[5] names\n\n<b>FF before the first\npoint, so one byte\nis both the index and\nthe not-started state</b>" as B <<out>>
rectangle "DONE\n<b>dmem[6]</b> = 1" as D <<out>>

rectangle "dmem[4]\n\nthe PREVIOUS pad\nlevel (00 or 40)\n\n<b>the edge detector,\nand it is a LEVEL\ncompare</b>" as P <<w>>
rectangle "dmem[7]\n\nthe PREVIOUS reading\nof the 1 us counter\n\n<b>elapsed = (now -\nprevious) mod 256</b>" as Q <<w>>

PW -[#3A7]-> T
PW -[#3A7]-> H
PW -[#3A7]-> P
P -[#3A7]-> T : a change to high RESETS both
P -[#3A7]-> H : a change to low STOPS H
Q -[#3A7]-> T : the elapsed time is added in
Q -[#3A7]-> H
T -[#3A7]-> B
H -[#3A7]-> B
B -[#3A7]-> D

note top of T
  <b>WHY T IS RESET AT EVERY RISING EDGE IS THE WHOLE
  PROGRAM.</b>

  The period could be computed as (now - last rising), but
  that is a 16-BIT SUBTRACT and the ISA has no carry flag,
  so the borrow of a-b cannot be read off the difference:
  (a-b) mod 256 has two preimages, one positive and one
  negative, for every value except zero. Getting it
  exactly needs a four-case analysis on bit 7, and a
  COMPARE is the same analysis again.

  Resetting instead turns the question from "how long since
  the last edge" into "how long since the edge that
  started this one", which the counter answers by itself.
  <b>Two resets remove every subtract, every compare and
  every divide.</b>
end note

note bottom of H
  <b>AND WHY H IS ALSO RESET AT THE RISING EDGE.</b>

  Same reason, different edge: H accumulates while the
  line is high and simply STOPS when it goes low, so the
  high time is whatever is in H at the next rising edge.
  No subtraction, no comparison, and no divide - the duty
  falls out of the pair rather than being computed.
end note

rectangle "THE TWELVE BANKED POINTS, as measured on real RTL" as HDR <<hi>>

rectangle "point 0\n\n<b>8 000 us</b>  125.00 Hz\nhigh <b>6 000 us</b>\nduty <b>75.0 %</b>" as P0 <<out>>
rectangle "point 1\n\n<b>10 001 us</b>  99.99 Hz\nhigh <b>2 500 us</b>\nduty <b>25.0 %</b>\n\n<b>THE 16-BIT CLAIM:</b> the low\nbyte wrapped 39 times" as P1 <<hi>>
rectangle "point 2\n\n<b>3 150 us</b>  317.46 Hz\nhigh <b>1 039 us</b>\nduty <b>33.0 %</b>" as P2 <<out>>
rectangle "point 3\n\n<b>4 000 us</b>  250.00 Hz\nhigh <b>400 us</b>\nduty <b>10.0 %</b>" as P3 <<out>>
rectangle "point 4\n\n<b>1 600 us</b>  625.00 Hz\nhigh <b>1 056 us</b>\nduty <b>66.0 %</b>" as P4 <<out>>
rectangle "point 5\n\n<b>2 000 us</b>  500.00 Hz\nhigh <b>1 600 us</b>\nduty <b>80.0 %</b>" as P5 <<out>>
rectangle "point 6\n\n<b>801 us</b>  1 248.44 Hz\nhigh <b>480 us</b>\nduty <b>59.9 %</b>" as P6 <<w>>
rectangle "point 7\n\n<b>1 000 us</b>  1 000.00 Hz\nhigh <b>399 us</b>\nduty <b>39.9 %</b>" as P7 <<w>>
rectangle "point 8\n\n<b>400 us</b>  2 500.00 Hz\nhigh <b>180 us</b>\nduty <b>45.0 %</b>" as P8 <<w>>
rectangle "point 9\n\n<b>500 us</b>  2 000.00 Hz\nhigh <b>275 us</b>\nduty <b>55.0 %</b>" as P9 <<w>>
rectangle "point 10\n\n<b>80 us</b>  12 500.00 Hz\nhigh <b>12 us</b>\nduty <b>15.0 %</b>" as P10 <<w>>
rectangle "point 11\n\n<b>100 us</b>  10 000.00 Hz\nhigh <b>50 us</b>\nduty <b>50.0 %</b>" as P11 <<w>>

note top of P1
  <b>Why point 1 is the act.</b>

  At 100 Hz the period is 10 000 us, which does not fit in
  one 8-bit counter. A firmware that counts into a byte
  wraps and reports <b>16 us</b> for a 100 Hz signal: a wrong
  ANSWER rather than a wrong precision, and a defect
  with <b>no symptom at any other point in the sweep</b>.

  So the counters are 16-bit, and the mutation gate
  perturbs the high byte specifically.
end note

note bottom of P6
  <b>Three points are out by EXACTLY ONE TICK, and they are
  printed as out rather than folded into a tolerance.</b>

  10 001 against 10 000, 801 against 800, 399 against 400
  - and the duty follows to a tenth of a point (59.9,
  39.9) because the two counts share the same edge
  discipline.

  <b>That is the resolution showing up in the log instead
  of hiding in a tolerance,</b> which is what a
  measured-truth record should look like. A figure or a
  page that printed "within 1 %" here would be hiding
  the instrument's resolution in the one place a reader
  wants to see it.
end note

note right of P8
  The first six points are runs 0 and 1 (durations above
  800 us); the last six are runs 2 to 5 (down to 80 us).
  <b>The sweep is deliberately wide:</b> 158 Hz to 10 kHz
  and ten different duties, so a firmware that is right at
  one end and wrong at the other cannot pass by averaging.
end note

@enduml
